  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 411 AM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nSunday, the shortwave will be east of the region, leaving zonal flow \nparked mainly to our north as a cold front approaches and arrives in \nsouthern Indiana during the evening hours. This could initiate \nanother round of isolated to scattered showers that would push east \nover and out of the region. Overall moisture levels ahead of the \nfront are expected to climb back into the 1.6 to 1.8 inch PWAT \nrange. Convective parameters look a lot like today's, so strong and \nsevere thunderstorms aren't expected before precipitation ends \nearly Sunday night.\n\nBy Monday, an upper ridge with surface high pressure behind the cold \nfront moves in over the Lower Ohio Valley. This will bring clearing \nskies and a return of WAA for Tuesday as the center of the surface \nhigh passes. This will lift highs back into the low to mid 90s \nbeginning Tuesday.  \n\nWednesday, as the upper ridge positively tilts to the east into New \nEngland, it will leave zonal flow north of the Ohio River with upper \nhigh pressure parked over the South. A surface high is expected to \nget pushed east along the zonal flow. This is expected to push a \nwest to east oriented front to near the Ohio River which could set \nup and become quasi-stationary, leading to low precipitation chances \nthrough at least the end of the work week.",
